    HTTP Security Headers Analysis

 1. Objective

Inspect HTTP response headers to identify security-related controls and understand their purposes.

     2. Target

* Website: https://www.linkedin.com/
* Tool: curl
* Environment:** Kali Linux
* Request type:** HTTP HEAD request

      3. Command Used

bash
curl -sS -I --max-time 15 https://www.linkedin.com/


     4. Results

The server returned `HTTP/2 200`, indicating a successful HTTP response.

The following security-related headers were observed:

| Header                       | Observed value                  | Purpose                                                                          |
| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Strict-Transport-Security    | max-age=31536000                | Enforces HTTPS for the specified duration after the browser receives the policy. |
| Content-Security-Policy      | Present                         | Defines permitted sources and restrictions for page resources.                   |
| X-Content-Type-Options       | nosniff                         | Prevents MIME-type sniffing.                                                     |
| X-Frame-Options              | SAMEORIGIN                      | Restricts framing to the same origin.                                            |
| Referrer-Policy              | strict-origin-when-cross-origin | Limits referrer information sent to other origins.                               |
| X-XSS-Protection             | 0                               | Disables the legacy browser XSS filter.                                          |
| Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy   | same-origin-allow-popups        | Controls isolation between browsing contexts.                                    |
| Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy | same-origin                     | Restricts certain cross-origin resource access.                                  |

    5. Analysis

The response included multiple security-related headers, including Content-Security-Policy, Strict-Transport-Security, and X-Content-Type-Options.

These headers provide browser-side security controls that can help reduce certain classes of attacks when correctly configured.

The presence of these headers alone does not establish that the website is secure. A comprehensive assessment would require additional authorized testing.

    6. Limitations

* This exercise inspected response headers only.
* It did not test for exploitable vulnerabilities.
* The findings represent one observation at one point in time.
* The analysis did not verify the complete effectiveness of each policy.
* Cookie values and session identifiers were excluded from this report.

      7. Conclusion

This exercise demonstrated how curl can be used to inspect HTTP response headers and document security-related controls.

It improved my understanding of HTTP, browser security policies, and basic web security assessment techniques.
